# Custom DnD Builder — server environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Every variable the server reads is listed here, and a test
# (src/server/env.test.ts) fails if this file and `src/server/env.ts` ever name different sets —
# so a variable added to the code without a line here is a failing build, not a support ticket.
#
# How this file reaches the server:
#   yarn dev / yarn build — `vite.config.ts` loads it into `process.env` (Vite does not do this on
#                           its own; it only exposes VITE_-prefixed keys to the *client*)
#   production            — the operator's runner supplies them, e.g. `node --env-file=.env …`.
#                           TICKET-POL-03 documents the run command.
#
# There is deliberately **no variable naming the backend** (v3 Req 47.7): the server hosts the
# client bundle, the API is addressed by relative path, and the socket derives its URL from
# window.location. A variable that could point somewhere else is a variable someone will
# eventually point somewhere else.

# DATABASE_URL — REQUIRED
# Path to the SQLite file holding every piece of server state. Relative paths resolve from the
# working directory. WAL mode means it is really three files (`app.db`, `-wal`, `-shm`), so the
# directory must be durable and is what a backup copies — see the README.
DATABASE_URL=./data/app.db

# NODE_ENV — optional, defaults to `development`
# Which build this is. The tooling sets it, and Vite refuses `NODE_ENV=production` from this file
# on purpose — a production build is `yarn build`, not a variable. It is listed because the server
# reads it; you should not normally need to set it.
NODE_ENV=development

# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET — REQUIRED (TICKET-AUTH-01)
# The key every Auth_Session cookie is signed with. Generate one with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# Changing it invalidates every cookie, which is also the way to sign everybody out at once.
# Keep it out of version control — `.env` is gitignored and this file holds a placeholder.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-base64-32

# AUTH_SESSION_DAYS — optional, defaults to 7
# How long an Auth_Session lasts. The cookie outlives the browser process, so closing the tab does
# not sign you out (v3 Req 48.1). TICKET-AUTH-04 turns this into the *idle* half of a rolling
# lifetime and adds an absolute ceiling beside it; it is a variable now so that ticket changes
# values rather than shape.
AUTH_SESSION_DAYS=7

# AUTH_SIGNIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS — optional, defaults to 5
# How many failed sign-ins one email address may make inside the window below before it is refused
# (v3 Req 30.7). The limit is per address rather than per IP, because the attack it exists against
# is guessing one person's password. Set to 0 to disable.
AUTH_SIGNIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5

# AUTH_SIGNIN_WINDOW_SECONDS — optional, defaults to 900
# The window those attempts are counted over, in seconds. 900 is fifteen minutes.
AUTH_SIGNIN_WINDOW_SECONDS=900
